\ Locals read as null before their first assignment, and the compiler's scan
\ for a body's local names sees the whole definition however the source
\ arrives (this file comes in on stdin line by line, so every multi-line
\ definition makes the scan read past a line boundary).

\ === null before first assignment ===

: dead-branch 0 if 5 to x then x ;
dead-branch . cr                               \ null
dead-branch none? . cr                         \ 1

: taken-branch 1 if 5 to x then x ;
taken-branch . cr                              \ 5

: zero-trip-index 7 5 1 do k loop k ;
zero-trip-index . cr                           \ 7 ((do) stores the index even when the loop runs zero times)

: headless-scratch [ 1 2 3 ] [: drop y 5 to y :] map ;
headless-scratch . cr                          \ [ null null null ] (fresh frame per iteration)

: head-scratch [ 1 2 3 ] [: i | i drop y 5 to y :] map ;
head-scratch . cr                              \ [ null 5 5 ] (head quotations keep one frame across the loop)

clear
\ === hoisting across chunk boundaries ===

: shadow-to
  5 to max
  max ;
shadow-to . cr                                 \ 5 (to on a word name shadows, same as file-loaded)

: shadow-do
  0 2 1 do min
    min .
  loop cr ;
shadow-do                                      \ 0 1

: branch-first-local
  dup if 3 to hx then
  0= if "none" . else hx . then cr ;
1 branch-first-local                           \ 3
0 branch-first-local                           \ none

clear
\ === evaluate never refills from the input stream ===

": eval-word 1 to ex ex 1 + ;" evaluate
eval-word . cr                                 \ 2
[: ": partial 1 to" evaluate :] catch . :message @ . cr   \ 1 to: expected a name
